<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:local="clr-namespace:FastiraChart">

    <!--
        Default style and template for FastiraChartControl.

        The border establishes the component's visible surface and applies
        BorderThickness only. It deliberately does NOT bind Padding: the control
        applies padding itself in ArrangeOverride, as part of computing the plot
        area, and arranges PART_ContentHost onto the result. Binding Padding here
        as well would subtract it twice. The same holds for MinimumAxisSpace: it is
        applied in ArrangeOverride and must not be bound here either.

        PART_ContentHost is deliberately an empty Grid: it makes the plot area real
        and measurable without committing to any layer structure. It covers the plot
        area only, never the axis bands; a host or a subclass may put content there.

        Every other part is a layer host, into which the control places a single
        drawing element; there is deliberately no element per tick, label, line or
        point. The parts' order in the root Grid is their z-order, and that order is
        the contract:

            Border                  plot background
            PART_GridHost           major grid
            PART_ReferenceLineHost  reference lines, then origin lines
            PART_SeriesHost         series, in collection order
            PART_ContentHost        plot content put there by a host or a subclass
            PART_LensHost           lens overlay and the chart inside it
            PART_InteractionHost    crosshair, then tooltip
            PART_AxisHost           tick marks, then labels

        So the grid is never painted over a tick or a label; a reference or origin
        line is drawn over a grid line at the same value; a series is drawn over the
        grid, reference and origin lines; the lens covers the main plot content
        beneath it; the crosshair is drawn over the series and the lens it reads; and
        nothing is ever drawn over a tick or a label. The tooltip is kept inside the
        plot.

        Every layer host spans the whole control, so each element is placed by the
        very expressions its tick and grid line are. ClipToPlotArea clips the series
        to the plot; with it off, the series is not clipped by the control at all.
        The lens takes no part in the layout: showing, moving or resizing it never
        moves the plot. The drawing hosts are not hit-testable: the overlay never
        takes a press, a wheel or a move from the chart.

        A replacement template need not supply every part. A missing part does not
        fail: the layer it hosts is not drawn (a missing PART_LensHost draws no
        lens; a missing PART_InteractionHost draws no crosshair or tooltip, and
        every gesture still works).

        Ticks are drawn only inside the reserved axis bands; grid, origin and
        reference lines only inside the plot.

        No Width, Height, MinWidth, MinHeight, MaxWidth or MaxHeight is set. The
        control must not request a size of its own, and the WPF defaults (Width and
        Height NaN, Min 0, Max infinity, alignment Stretch) are already correct.
        Setting them here would only make them harder for a host to override.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="{x:Type local:FastiraChartControl}">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="#FF1B1B1F" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="#FF3A3A42" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
        <!--
            Tick marks are stroked with Foreground. Reusing an existing Control property
            keeps them stylable without a tick-specific styling API.
        -->
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#FFA8A8B4" />
        <!--
            Major grid: visible against the default background, well below the tick and label
            brush so the grid reads as a guide and not as data.
        -->
        <Setter Property="MajorGridBrush" Value="#FF34343E" />
        <!--
            Origin and reference lines. The origin is a brighter neutral than the grid, and at
            1.5 DIP heavier, so the line that means zero reads above the grid line it
            lies on; it stays below the tick and label brush so it is not mistaken for
            an axis. Reference lines take a muted warm accent, so a caller's marker is
            told apart from both the neutral grid and the neutral origin by hue.
        -->
        <Setter Property="OriginLineBrush" Value="#FF6E6E7C" />
        <Setter Property="ReferenceLineBrush" Value="#FFB08A4E" />
        <!--
            Series fallback stroke, for a LineSeries that names no
            Stroke of its own: a saturated blue that reads as data against the default background and is
            told apart by hue from the neutral grid and origin and the warm
            reference lines.
        -->
        <Setter Property="SeriesStroke" Value="#FF4FA3E8" />
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="{x:Type local:FastiraChartControl}">
                    <!--
                        The parts are SIBLINGS in a root Grid, not nested.

                        That is deliberate and load-bearing. The control arranges
                        PART_ContentHost itself, in the control's own coordinate space,
                        offsetting by BorderThickness to land inside the border. That
                        arithmetic is only correct while the part's visual parent spans the
                        whole control. Nesting the part inside the Border would inset its
                        parent by the border thickness and silently subtract it twice.

                        The Border therefore paints background and border only and has no
                        child. PART_AxisHost spans the whole control so that tick marks,
                        which live outside the plot in the reserved axis bands, share the same
                        coordinate space the control computes them in. PART_GridHost spans it
                        for the same reason: a grid line is computed in that space by the same
                        expression as its tick, and only the drawing is clipped to the plot.

                        No Padding binding here by design, and none for MinimumAxisSpace either: see
                        the note above.
                    -->
                    <Grid>
                        <Border Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                                BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                                BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                                SnapsToDevicePixels="True" />
                        <Grid x:Name="PART_GridHost" IsHitTestVisible="False" />
                        <Grid x:Name="PART_ReferenceLineHost" IsHitTestVisible="False" />
                        <Grid x:Name="PART_SeriesHost" IsHitTestVisible="False" />
                        <Grid x:Name="PART_ContentHost" />
                        <Grid x:Name="PART_LensHost" IsHitTestVisible="False" />
                        <Grid x:Name="PART_InteractionHost" IsHitTestVisible="False" />
                        <Grid x:Name="PART_AxisHost" IsHitTestVisible="False" />
                    </Grid>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
